        "evidence": "Block titles are used as bare noun subjects throughout the second and third paragraphs, so each sentence's head noun arrives only at the end of a long adjunct chain: \"The structural pivot asserts that ...\", \"The literature alignment ties this picture to ...\", \"The product factorization theorem splits ...\", \"The transition-operator proposition interprets ...\", \"The trace-collapse lemma identifies ...\". Eight sentences in a row share the same subject-verb-object shape and the same length, which is the flattened cadence rather than the syntax, but the title-as-subject construction is what makes them stall aloud.",
